  "kind": "tea",
  "teaId": "anna-temple",
  "text": "She waited decades in the temple and recognised him the second he walked through the door.\n\nLuke two. Anna. Prophetess. Daughter of Phanuel, tribe of Aser. Luke says she was of a great age, married seven years, and then a widow of about fourscore and four years.\n\nQuick honesty note. Translators split on that. It either means she was eighty-four, or she'd been widowed for eighty-four years. Both readings are a very long wait, and anybody who picks the one that preaches better is doing you dirty.\n\nWhat is not ambiguous is how she spent it. She departed not from the temple, but served God with fastings and prayers night and day.\n\nNight and day. For decades. In a building, waiting.\n\nAnd then one ordinary morning a poor couple carries a newborn in to do what the law required. Nothing about it looks special.\n\nAnd Anna comes in that instant.\n\nShe gives thanks to God, and she spake of him to all them that looked for redemption in Jerusalem.\n\nDecades of showing up. And she knew him immediately, and she started telling everybody who would listen.\n\nPatience is not passivity.",
